    <text>
      <body>
         <div type="doc">
            <head>
               <ref target="https://dodis.ch/53168">dodis.ch/53168</ref>
               <title type="main">Draft memo<note xml:id="e47">Draft memo (translated from Polish):
                        <idno>Archives of the Polish Ministry of Foreign Affairs AMSZ, Dep. IV
                        31/92, w. 3.</idno>
                        </note> by the Director of the 4<hi>th</hi> Department of
                  the Polish Ministry of Foreign Affairs, <persName key="P53484">Janusz Fekecz</persName>
                    </title>
               <title type="sub">Memorandum Concerning the Citizens of the German Democratic
                  Republic who Remain at the Embassy of the Federal Republic of Germany in
                  Warsaw</title>
            </head>
            <opener>
               <add>Confidential</add>
               <dateline>Warsaw, 14 September 1989</dateline>
            </opener>
            <p n="1">I. On 13 September, Minister <persName key="Skubiszewski">K. Skubiszewski</persName> received the Director of the MFA’s Political
               Department <persName key="P57383">Kastrup</persName>, who arrived in Poland as Minister <persName key="P15414">Genscher</persName>’s special envoy to discuss the group of <choice>
                        <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                        <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                    </choice> citizens
               remaining at the <choice>
                        <abbr>FRG</abbr>
                        <expan>Federal Republic of Germany</expan>
                    </choice> Embassy in Warsaw. The talks were also attended by Ambassador
                  <persName key="P57583">Schoeller</persName> and Deputy Director of the MFA’s 4<hi>th</hi>
               Department <persName key="P57584">Sułek</persName>.</p>
            <p>Director Kastrup explained that the aim of his trip to Poland was to hold talks with
               over 50 <choice>
                        <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                        <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                    </choice> citizens who are currently at the <choice>
                        <abbr>FRG</abbr>
                        <expan>Federal Republic of Germany</expan>
                    </choice> Embassy in Warsaw and seeking to
               leave for the <choice>
                        <abbr>FRG</abbr>
                        <expan>Federal Republic of Germany</expan>
                    </choice> for a permanent stay, and to present the official position of the
               West German side to the Polish authorities.</p>
            <p>He referred to a conversation between Minister Genscher and <persName key="P57585">L. Wałęsa</persName> held in Bonn on
               8 September, during which the West German foreign minister praised the Hungarian
               authorities for their approach to the departure of <choice>
                        <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                        <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                    </choice> citizens from the <choice>
                        <abbr>FRG</abbr>
                        <expan>Federal Republic of Germany</expan>
                    </choice> Embassy
               in Budapest and the Hungarian territory for the <choice>
                        <abbr>FRG</abbr>
                        <expan>Federal Republic of Germany</expan>
                    </choice> via Austria, and requested L.
               Wałęsa, as a representative of the new Poland, to apply “the Hungarian solution” to
               the group of <choice>
                        <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                        <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                    </choice> citizens remaining at the <choice>
                        <abbr>FRG</abbr>
                        <expan>Federal Republic of Germany</expan>
                    </choice> Embassy in Warsaw. At the same time,
               Minister Genscher emphasized the historic importance of this issue for the West-East
               relationship in Europe, and to human rights in general. According to Kastrup, L.
               Wałęsa promised Genscher that the “Hungarian model” would be followed in Poland.</p>
            <p>Director Kastrup made it clear that the <choice>
                        <abbr>FRG</abbr>
                        <expan>Federal Republic of Germany</expan>
                    </choice> would not set out German legal positions
               in the case at hand (“it would be irrelevant which travel documents entitled the <choice>
                        <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                        <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                    </choice>
               citizens to leave Poland”). The <choice>
                        <abbr>FRG</abbr>
                        <expan>Federal Republic of Germany</expan>
                    </choice> would prefer a pragmatic solution that took
               account of humanitarian considerations. To that end, they would be ready to get the
               Polish Red Cross, the German Red Cross and possibly also the International Committee
               of the Red Cross in Geneva on board. Both Kastrup and Ambassador Schoeller expressed
               their hope that the Polish authorities would show understanding and support for West
               German efforts to arrange the departure of <choice>
                        <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                        <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                    </choice> citizens for the <choice>
                        <abbr>FRG</abbr>
                        <expan>Federal Republic of Germany</expan>
                    </choice>. They emphasized
               that the focus of attention of the West German public and media was on the <choice>
                        <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                        <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                    </choice>
               citizens at the <choice>
                        <abbr>FRG</abbr>
                        <expan>Federal Republic of Germany</expan>
                    </choice> Embassy in Warsaw, just as it was for those at the <choice>
                        <abbr>FRG</abbr>
                        <expan>Federal Republic of Germany</expan>
                    </choice> Embassy in
               Prague. In reply to Minister Skubiszewski’s question, Director Kastrup described the
               Hungarian authorities’ decision to allow <choice>
                        <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                        <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                    </choice> citizens to leave for the <choice>
                        <abbr>FRG</abbr>
                        <expan>Federal Republic of Germany</expan>
                    </choice> en masse
               as a “humanitarian gesture in a European spirit”. In this context, he recalled
               Genscher’s words to the Hungarian authorities: “We will never forget what you
               did.”</p>
            <p>Minister Skubiszewski observed that the Polish side was also in favour of a pragmatic
               and humanitarian solution to the issue of the <choice>
                        <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                        <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                    </choice> citizens remaining at the <choice>
                        <abbr>FRG</abbr>
                        <expan>Federal Republic of Germany</expan>
                    </choice>
               Embassy in Warsaw. From our standpoint, the best option would be an amicable
               settlement reached by the East and West German authorities, one that would respect
               the interests of both governments as well as the people concerned. Poland advocated
               the observance of human rights, including the principle of free flow of people. In
               the case at hand, however, we had to bear in mind the good of Poland’s relations with
               West and East Germany. He explained that he had not had the opportunity to
               familiarize himself with the content of Minister Genscher’s conversation with L.
               Wałęsa. The stance of the Polish authorities would be determined by arrangements to
               be made directly by the governments of the two German states with respect to the <choice>
                        <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                        <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                    </choice>
               citizens remaining at the <choice>
                        <abbr>FRG</abbr>
                        <expan>Federal Republic of Germany</expan>
                    </choice> Embassy in Warsaw.</p>
            <p>Director Kastrup recalled that the <choice>
                        <abbr>FRG</abbr>
                        <expan>Federal Republic of Germany</expan>
                    </choice> had held talks with the <choice>
                        <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                        <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                    </choice> about the East
               German citizens staying at the <choice>
                        <abbr>FRG</abbr>
                        <expan>Federal Republic of Germany</expan>
                    </choice> Permanent Representation in Berlin and at <choice>
                        <abbr>FRG</abbr>
                        <expan>Federal Republic of Germany</expan>
                    </choice>
               diplomatic missions accredited in socialist countries. In contrast to the
               arrangements that had regulated similar cases in the past, this time the <choice>
                        <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                        <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                    </choice>
               authorities were reluctant to issue a “silent guarantee” allowing such people to
               leave for the <choice>
                        <abbr>FRG</abbr>
                        <expan>Federal Republic of Germany</expan>
                    </choice> (the <choice>
                        <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                        <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                    </choice>’s policy at the time had been to “consider favourably”
               applications for an exit permit). This time round, the <choice>
                        <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                        <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                    </choice> would only be willing to
               guarantee its citizens staying at the Embassies in Prague and Warsaw (until recently
               also Budapest) that after their return to the <choice>
                        <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                        <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                    </choice>, they would avoid punishment or
               consequences at their workplace, and would be granted representation by a lawyer when
               they appeared before East German administrative authorities. What the <choice>
                        <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                        <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                    </choice> authorities
               would not accept was that such citizens should be able to leave a third state
               directly for the <choice>
                        <abbr>FRG</abbr>
                        <expan>Federal Republic of Germany</expan>
                    </choice>, as had been happening under “the Hungarian solution”.
               Consequently, people who were staying at the <choice>
                        <abbr>FRG</abbr>
                        <expan>Federal Republic of Germany</expan>
                    </choice> Embassy in Warsaw could only have “a
               very vague hope” of leaving for West Germany. In Kastrup’s view, the <choice>
                        <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                        <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                    </choice> authorities
               were unlikely to change their position at that stage, which was why talks between
               both German states had stalled.</p>
            <p>Minister Skubiszewski concluded the meeting by reiterating that we appreciated the
               importance of the humanitarian aspect involved in the issue of <choice>
                        <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                        <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                    </choice> citizens remaining
               at the <choice>
                        <abbr>FRG</abbr>
                        <expan>Federal Republic of Germany</expan>
                    </choice> Embassy in Warsaw, and were aware of the possible ramifications for our
               relations with West and East Germany. He promised to look into the matter and give a
               concrete reply setting out the Polish position.</p>
            <p/>
            <p n="1">II. On 13 September, Director Kastrup and Ambassador Schoeller were also
               received by <persName key="P44031">J. Czyrek</persName>, the
               Minister of State at the President’s Office. One of the topics discussed was the
               problem of over 50 <choice>
                        <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                        <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                    </choice> citizens staying at the <choice>
                        <abbr>FRG</abbr>
                        <expan>Federal Republic of Germany</expan>
                    </choice> Embassy in Warsaw and wanting to
               leave for West Germany.</p>
            <p>Director Kastrup emphasized that he was aware of the fundamental differences on legal
               matters between the People’s Republic of Poland and the Federal Republic of Germany.
               Even so, the West German side sought a pragmatic solution with humanitarian
               dimensions. Minister Czyrek asked for an explanation of the political context of the
               problem, given recent declarations by the Federal Republic of Germany that it was not
               interested in destabilizing the <choice>
                        <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                        <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                    </choice>, and was keen to ensure the appropriate standards
               in that country in order to stem emigration. Did the current situation point to a
               shift in West German policy? (The <choice>
                        <abbr>FRG</abbr>
                        <expan>Federal Republic of Germany</expan>
                    </choice> was creating many incentives to encourage
               immigration). K<add type="edition">[astrup]</add> emphasized that “human matters”
               could not be separated from politics. Young East Germans saw no opportunities for
               themselves (this went beyond mere economic motivation); they had been following news
               about the progress of reforms in the <choice>
                        <abbr>USSR</abbr>
                        <expan>Union of Soviet Socialist Republics</expan>
                    </choice>, Poland and Hungary. Meanwhile, the current
               East German leadership was unable to solve these problems. “Mass fleeing” had its
               roots solely in the <choice>
                        <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                        <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                    </choice>, so a policy change in the <choice>
                        <abbr>FRG</abbr>
                        <expan>Federal Republic of Germany</expan>
                    </choice> was not a factor here. K<add type="edition">[astrup]</add> underscored that he was requesting “humanitarian
               assistance in solving this problem, while taking account of human rights”. Minister
               Czyrek replied that he appreciated the humanitarian dimension of the issue. We would
               not try to “turn it into a problem”. From the logical and substantive points of view,
               this was primarily a matter to be settled between East and West Germany, however, and
               as such should be resolved by way of an understanding between the two countries. Such
               an understanding would be decisive for Poland, as we could be loyal both to the <choice>
                        <abbr>FRG</abbr>
                        <expan>Federal Republic of Germany</expan>
                    </choice>
               and <choice>
                        <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                        <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                    </choice>. Minister Czyrek referred to Minister Skubiszewski’s decision as presented
               during the talks with K<add type="edition">[astrup]</add> , whereby a working group
               would be set up as a matter of urgency to draft proposals for a Polish position.</p>
            <p>Minister Czyrek went on to underline that the matter was very difficult for Poland,
               as we viewed our friendly relations with the <choice>
                        <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                        <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                    </choice> as a serious issue and, unlike
               Hungary, we have no direct border with any Western states.</p>
            <p/>
            <p n="1">III. Conclusions</p>
            <p>a) The issue of <choice>
                        <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                        <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                    </choice> citizens remaining at the <choice>
                        <abbr>FRG</abbr>
                        <expan>Federal Republic of Germany</expan>
                    </choice> Embassy in Warsaw could have
               negative repercussions for either of the two German states, depending on which
               solution we support. If we side with the <choice>
                        <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                        <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                    </choice>, we risk negative effects in the context
               of <persName key="P31852">Kohl</persName>’s visit to Poland<note xml:id="e156">Helmut Kohl
                  visited Poland on 9–10 and 12–14 November 1989 (following the fall of the Berlin
                  Wall, Chancellor Kohl interrupted his visit to go back to the <choice>
                            <abbr>FRG</abbr>
                            <expan>Federal Republic of Germany</expan>
                        </choice>).</note>; if we
               go along with West German proposals, the political fallout could affect our
               relationship with the <choice>
                        <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                        <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                    </choice>.</p>
            <p>b) From our point of view, the best way out would be if the two German states
               achieved an amicable settlement between themselves. At this stage, however, such a
               solution is unlikely and we are under permanent pressure from representatives of both
               countries (see <persName key="P50485">J. Mąkosa</persName>’s memo of 12 September on the conversation with <choice>
                        <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                        <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                    </choice> Ambassador <persName key="P57587">J.
               van Zwoll</persName>).</p>
            <p>c) In these circumstances, the MFA has set up a working group headed by <persName key="P50550">B.
                  Kulski</persName> to come up with possible solutions. What could prove helpful for
               us would be a solution to be adopted in the next few days in the similar case in
               Prague.</p>
            <p>d) One should bear in mind that the problem will continue to grow (the number of <choice>
                        <abbr>GDR</abbr>
                        <expan>German Democratic Republic</expan>
                    </choice>
               citizens at the <choice>
                        <abbr>FRG</abbr>
                        <expan>Federal Republic of Germany</expan>
                    </choice> Embassy in Warsaw increased to 50 in the course of two
               weeks).</p>
         </div>
